{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"arrays-span","title":"Arrays, std::array & std::span","group":"Pointers, Arrays & Strings","order":21,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C arrays are fixed-size blocks that decay to pointers. <code>std::array</code> is the same thing with value semantics and a size; <code>std::span</code> is a non-owning view over any contiguous sequence.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>C-style arrays</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;iterator&gt;\n\nint main() {\n    int a[5] = {1, 2, 3};                 // remaining elements are zero-initialised\n    int b[] = {4, 5, 6};                  // size deduced: 3\n    int grid[2][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};\n\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a[3] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::size(b) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sizeof(a) / sizeof(a[0]) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; grid[1][2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    for (int x : b) std::cout &lt;&lt; x;       // range-for works: the size is part of the type\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>0 3 5\n6\n456</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>An array passed to a function <strong>decays</strong> to a pointer — <code>sizeof</code> inside the function gives the pointer size, and bounds are never checked. Arrays can't be copied or assigned with <code>=</code> either.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i><code>std::array&lt;T, N&gt;</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;array&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\nstd::array&lt;int, 3&gt; doubled(std::array&lt;int, 3&gt; arr) {   // passed &amp; returned by value — just works\n    for (int&amp; x : arr) x *= 2;\n    return arr;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::array&lt;int, 5&gt; a{5, 3, 9, 1, 7};\n    std::sort(a.begin(), a.end());\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a.size() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; a.front() &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; a.back() &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto copy = a;                 // arrays are copyable\n    copy[0] = 100;\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; a[0] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; (a == copy) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    auto d = doubled({1, 2, 3});\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; d[2] &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n\n    try {\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; a.at(10) &lt;&lt; '\\n';      // bounds-checked access\n    } catch (const std::out_of_range&amp; e) {\n        std::cout &lt;&lt; \"out_of_range\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>5 1 9\n1 0\n6\nout_of_range</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>C array <code>T[N]</code></th><th><code>std::array&lt;T,N&gt;</code></th><th><code>std::vector&lt;T&gt;</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Size</td><td>Fixed, compile time</td><td>Fixed, compile time</td><td>Dynamic</td></tr><tr><td>Storage</td><td>Inline (stack/static)</td><td>Inline (stack/static)</td><td>Heap</td></tr><tr><td>Knows its size</td><td>Only in scope of declaration</td><td><code>.size()</code></td><td><code>.size()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Copy / compare with <code>=</code> <code>==</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Decays to pointer</td><td>Yes</td><td>No (<code>.data()</code> if needed)</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Bounds check</td><td>Never</td><td><code>.at()</code></td><td><code>.at()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-horizontal\"></i><code>std::span</code> (C++20)</h3>\n<p>A <code>span&lt;T&gt;</code> is a pointer + length — a cheap <em>view</em>. Write one function that accepts a C array, <code>std::array</code> or <code>std::vector</code>:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;array&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;span&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nint sum(std::span&lt;const int&gt; xs) {\n    int s = 0;\n    for (int x : xs) s += x;\n    return s;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    int raw[] = {1, 2, 3};\n    std::array&lt;int, 2&gt; arr{10, 20};\n    std::vector&lt;int&gt; vec{100, 200, 300, 400};\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sum(raw) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sum(arr) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sum(vec) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    std::span&lt;int&gt; part(vec.data() + 1, 2);        // a sub-range\n    part[0] = 0;                                    // writes through to vec\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; sum(part) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; vec[1] &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; sum(std::span(vec).first(2)) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 30 1000\n300 0 100</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Like any view, a <code>span</code> doesn't own memory — it dangles if the vector reallocates or is destroyed.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why can't you get the size of an array inside a function that receives it?</summary><div><p>Because <code>void f(int arr[])</code> is really <code>void f(int* arr)</code> — the array decayed to a pointer and the length isn't part of a pointer. Pass the size separately, take <code>std::array&lt;int, N&gt;&amp;</code>, a template <code>T (&amp;arr)[N]</code>, or a <code>std::span</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}